<body>
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
  <div class="site" id="top">
    <header class="header"><a class="brand" href="/#top" aria-label="BHIC home"><img src="/assets/7732233b3dc43e88.png" alt="Bugungu"></a><button class="menu-button" type="button" aria-label="Open navigation" aria-expanded="false"><span class="hamburger"></span></button></header>
    <aside class="drawer" aria-hidden="true"><div class="drawer__top"><img class="drawer__logo" src="/assets/7732233b3dc43e88.png" alt="Bugungu"><button class="drawer__close" type="button" aria-label="Close navigation">×</button></div><nav aria-label="Primary navigation"><a href="/">Home</a><a href="/about/" aria-current="page">About BHIC</a><a href="/heritage/">Heritage &amp; museum</a><a href="/visit/">Visit</a><a href="/contact/">Contact</a></nav><a class="button button--primary" href="/support/">Support BHIC</a></aside><div class="scrim"></div>
    <main id="main">
      <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="about-title"><figure class="hero__image"><img src="/assets/8ca7e953cb8e3402.webp" alt="Kiiza Wilson, founder of BHIC" fetchpriority="high"></figure><div class="hero__copy"><p class="eyebrow">In Kiiza Wilson’s words</p><h1 id="about-title">I refused to let Bugungu become a stranger to its own story.</h1><p class="hero__thesis">That refusal became the Bugungu Heritage and Information Centre.</p><p class="hero__identity">Kiiza Wilson · Founder of BHIC</p></div><a class="hero__scroll" href="#origin" aria-label="Read how BHIC began">↓</a></section>
      <section class="origin" id="origin"><span class="chapter-tag">01 · What I saw</span><figure class="origin__image" data-reveal><img src="/assets/614fa4866f260380.webp" alt="Kiiza Wilson explaining a Bugungu basket inside the museum" loading="lazy"></figure><div class="origin__copy" data-reveal><h2>Change was arriving faster than memory could travel.</h2><p>Roads, oil and new opportunities were reshaping Buliisa. Yet much of what makes Bugungu recognisable still lived in people—in names, songs, objects, skills and memories passed from one generation to another.</p><p>I knew development could move us forward while leaving part of us behind.</p><blockquote>“The question was not whether Buliisa would change. It was whether Bugungu would remain present inside that change.”<cite>Kiiza Wilson</cite></blockquote></div></section>
      <section class="work"><figure class="work__image"><img src="/assets/21ccbae93b6256a2.webp" alt="Kiiza Wilson sharing objects in the BHIC museum" loading="lazy"></figure><div class="work__copy" data-reveal><span class="chapter-tag">02 · What I began</span><p class="eyebrow">Memory needed a home</p><h2>So I began with what was close.</h2><p>Listening to people. Recording what they knew. Bringing together objects, maps, books and stories. The museum became a place where Bugungu could meet itself again—not as something finished, but as knowledge still in use.</p></div></section>
      <section class="turn" data-reveal><div class="turn__line"></div><blockquote>“What began as my concern could never remain my work alone.”</blockquote><p><strong>This is where “I” becomes “we.”</strong> Elders, cultural practitioners, young people, educators and neighbours gave the institution its meaning.</p></section>
      <section class="evidence" aria-labelledby="evidence-title"><div class="evidence__intro" data-reveal><span class="chapter-tag">03 · The work grows</span><h2 id="evidence-title">The clearest proof is participation.</h2><p>These are not decorative milestones. They show Bugungu knowledge moving into new hands and new public spaces.</p></div><div class="evidence__grid"><article class="evidence-card" data-reveal><figure class="evidence-card__image"><img src="/assets/821a23fad3813e01.webp" alt="Members of the Bugungu Heritage Troupe gathered with musical instruments" loading="lazy"></figure><div class="evidence-card__copy"><span class="evidence-card__year">2021</span><h3>58 young people join in.</h3><p>They take part in the Hoima radio heritage competition through BHIC, carrying local heritage into a new public space.</p></div></article><article class="evidence-card" data-reveal><figure class="evidence-card__image"><img src="/assets/d7e1ebb423278237.webp" alt="A visitor studies the Mpeehu za Bugungu community wind map" loading="lazy"></figure><div class="evidence-card__copy"><span class="evidence-card__year">2022</span><h3>Community knowledge becomes a map.</h3><p>Mpeehu za Bugungu records local winds through language, place and lived experience.</p></div></article></div><div class="registered" data-reveal><strong>Established</strong><p>BHIC has worked as a registered community heritage organisation since 2013.</p></div></section>
      <section class="shared"><figure class="shared__image"><img src="/assets/205af39cd4b5afd3.webp" alt="Kiiza Wilson seated with colleagues in Buliisa" loading="lazy"></figure><div class="shared__copy" data-reveal><span class="chapter-tag" style="color:#ffc277">04 · Carried together</span><h2>A founder can begin an institution. Only a community can make its memory live.</h2><p>Today, BHIC records living memory, cares for the museum, supports learning and advances Bugungu cultural rights. The work belongs to the people who hold, use and pass on that knowledge.</p></div></section>
      <section class="method"><figure class="method__image" data-reveal><img src="/assets/c35cdfc52417746d.webp" alt="Close view of a Bugungu drum being held inside the museum" loading="lazy"></figure><div class="method__copy" data-reveal><span class="chapter-tag">05 · How we work</span><h2>An object is never only an object.</h2><p>A drum carries material, skill, sound, ceremony and memory. BHIC keeps those relationships together rather than separating an object from the people who give it meaning.</p><div class="method__steps"><div class="method__step"><span>01</span><div><strong>Listen</strong><p>Knowledge holders help determine what the object means.</p></div></div><div class="method__step"><span>02</span><div><strong>Connect</strong><p>The object stays linked to its use, language and place.</p></div></div><div class="method__step"><span>03</span><div><strong>Keep it close</strong><p>Documentation strengthens Bugungu rather than removing ownership.</p></div></div></div></div></section>
      <section class="visit" id="visit"><figure class="visit__image"><img src="/assets/2cdc90f843308491.webp" alt="A visitor encountering a Bugungu drum at BHIC" loading="lazy"></figure><div class="visit__copy" data-reveal><p class="eyebrow">Your place in the story</p><h2>Now meet it in person.</h2><p>Come to BHIC in Buliisa. Encounter the objects, people and living knowledge in the place that gives them meaning.</p><a class="button button--primary" href="/visit/">Plan your visit</a><div class="visit__details"><span>Buliisa, Uganda</span><span>Contact the team before travelling</span></div></div></section>
    </main>
